> **1. Good signings exist (in Lean).** For every n ≥ 1, every nonempty multiset on Z_n has a good signing, unless it consists of two elements a, b with a − b not divisible by 2^k (so there is no 
> exception for odd n, and two equal elements are no exception); such a pair has none.
> - *Status.* In Lean, in the shared library `lean-tiling` (`agent2-pdtile/lean-tiling/` in the joint folder): `exists_signs_all` and `not_exists_signs_of_exceptionalPair` in `Pnq/EvenFinal.lean`; for 
> odd n, `exists_signs_odd` in `Pnq/SignFinal.lean`. In Lean the exception reads "two marks whose difference has even additive order". This is the same (the order of c in Z_n is n/gcd(n, c), which is 
> even exactly when 2^k ∤ c), but that is not a statement of the Lean library; Noon checked it by program for n ≤ 2,048. Noon read the final statements and the definition and tried them by 
> exhaustion with programs of its own; it did not read the proofs. Lean has not checked any share of good signings.
> - *Where.* Sections 12 and 14 (on paper, for odd n and for every n), 13 and 15 (in Lean).
> 
> **2. Twice (on paper; in Lean, not in the shared library).** Let n = 2^k·n′ with n′ odd, and B′ the reduction of B modulo n′ (a signing of B is a signing of B′, element by element). The 
> signings of B that are bad on Z_n number at most twice the signings of B′ that are bad on Z_(n′). So every multiset of m elements on every Z_n, even or odd, has a share of good signings of at 
> least 1 − 2·β(m).
> - *Status.* On paper, with a subtracted term that makes it sharper, and refereed. In Lean, not in the shared library: in my private copy of it, the two files posted in `lean-lift/union-lean/` 
> (`card_exists_badAt_le`, for exponents e_i ∈ ℕ in place of a multiset on Z_n, and without the subtracted term; the statement was written by me and audited by a fresh instance, which also proved 
> the bridge to multisets in a file of its own). By its post of 22:02 UTC, 27 November, Noon had not read it.
> - *Where.* Section 16.
> 
> **3. At least a quarter (on paper, with exact computations).** β(m) ≤ 3/8 for every m ≥ 3, with equality only for m = 4. More exactly: β(3), …, β(8) = 1/4, 3/8, 1/4, 11/32, 7/32, 39/128; 
> β(m) ≤ 1/4 for every odd m ≥ 3; β(m) ≤ 47/128 for even m ≥ 10. Combined with statement 2, on every Z_n: the share of good signings is at least 1/4 for m ≥ 3 and at least 1/2 for odd m ≥ 
> 3; the least share is exactly 1/2, 1/4, 1/2, 5/16, 9/16, 25/64 for m = 3, …, 8; and the quarter is attained by four elements ({0, 0, 0, 1} on Z_2, for example) and by no other number of elements.
> - *Status.* On paper, with finite computations in exact fractions that the proofs rest on: three texts by three fresh instances, each refereed and passed by a fresh instance of its own (the authors' 
> last changes of wording no referee has read). The quarter is proved in all three; the half for every odd m in the first alone (the third has it to m = 199); β(5), β(6), β(7) in the first and 
> third; β(8) in the third alone. The multisets on even moduli that attain the least shares for m = 5, …, 8 were found by my runs; the reader of section 17 recomputed all six examples by all 
> signings. Not in Lean.
> - *Where.* Section 17.
> 
